INT. OFFICE BREAK ROOM - DAY

Beige. A microwave with a taped sign: NOT A DISH SOAP DISPENSER. A fridge covered in passive-aggressive notes. A cake sits on the table, untouched.

DEBORAH PIERCE, fifties, hovers behind the cake with a knife. FELIX NWOSU, twenties, hugs a laptop. SHANTI VENKATARAMAN, thirties, stands at the coffee machine as if she has been standing there for a decade. KURT BJORKLUND, forties, leans in the doorway. And TEODORA IVANOVA, sixty, the birthday girl, sits at the table, smiling with the stiffness of a hostage.

DEBORAH
Okay, everybody! Cake time!

FELIX
(to himself)
Cake time.

SHANTI
(to Deborah)
I didn't know it was Teodora's birthday.

DEBORAH
It's not. It's her retirement.

TEODORA
It's both.

KURT
It's a hybrid.

DEBORAH
Teodora, how many years?

TEODORA
Thirty-one.

FELIX
Thirty-one?

SHANTI
I've been here eleven and I've never seen you.

TEODORA
I sit by the copier.

KURT
(to the room)
Does anyone have a plate?

DEBORAH
They're in the cupboard.

FELIX
They're not in the cupboard.

SHANTI
They're by the sink.

KURT
They're in the drawer.

Everyone starts at once. Deborah opens the cupboard, Felix checks the sink, Shanti pulls the drawer, Kurt says "I'll look in the fridge" and does not move. Teodora watches from the table, hands folded.

TEODORA
It's all right. I don't need a plate.

DEBORAH
Of course you do.

TEODORA
I brought my own.

She takes a small blue enamel plate from her bag and sets it on the table. The others stop. Nobody says a word.

FELIX
(muttering)
Thirty-one years.

DEBORAH
(to the group)
Let's sing!

They sing, ragged and sparse. Teodora lifts her plate with both hands as if receiving a sacrament. Deborah cuts the cake, hands her the first slice, and Teodora eats it slowly, looking at none of them.

KURT
(muttering)
Good cake.

SHANTI
(softly, to Teodora)
Thank you.

TEODORA
For what?

SHANTI
I don't know.

Teodora nods, as if that were exactly right, and takes another bite.
